# How this deck was made (W3C PROV-O): researched card by card from the sources below,
# each card checked against at least two of them, then in the review rounds below.
<#compilation>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-04T12:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:used <https://www.wikidata.org/> ,
              <https://archive.org/details/ERIC_ED096854> ,
              <https://dicionario.priberam.org/> ,
              <https://voc.cplp.org/> ,
              <https://pt.wiktionary.org/> ,
              <https://en.wiktionary.org/> ,
              <https://folkets-lexikon.csc.kth.se/folkets/> ;
    prov:wasAssociatedWith <#anton-wiklund> ;
    prov:wasInformedBy <#review-0> ,
                       <#review-1> ,
                       <#review-2> ,
                       <#review-3> ,
                       <#review-4> ,
                       <#review-5> ;
    rdfs:comment "Compiled by Anton Wiklund with the help of AI."@en ,
                 "Sammanställd av Anton Wiklund med hjälp av AI."@sv ,
                 "The cards were checked by machine (live Wikidata and the app's SHACL and DCAT-AP validators)."@en ,
                 "Variety and spelling. The deck follows European Portuguese (the norm of Portugal), spelt by the 1990 Orthographic Agreement as Portugal applies it, and prefers words common to Portugal and Brazil. Where the two differ for a card, the Brazilian form is named in a note (dezesseis, dezessete, dezenove; o cachorro; oi). European Portuguese was chosen because Wikidata's Portuguese lexemes for the numbers from 11 up are European (for example L1635008 dezesseis is glossed \"o mesmo que dezasseis\") and because the deck uses the Portuguese national orthographic vocabulary (VOP) and Priberam's European-norm view to check spelling. Words whose everyday form differs (o comboio and o trem, o pequeno-almoço and o café da manhã, castanho and marrom) were left out rather than given two answers."@en ,
                 "Word list. A candidate list for a first Portuguese course was drawn up by hand from the brief's categories (numbers 0–20, the tens and 100; days; months; colours; family; greetings and polite phrases; everyday nouns; adjectives; verbs), choosing the most basic word for each concept and following the authored French and Spanish decks where the languages allow. No frequency list or textbook word list was copied."@en ,
                 "Wikidata lexemes. SPARQL query 1 fetched every Portuguese lexeme (language Q5146) whose lemma is a candidate, with its category, gender (P5185), senses, English and Portuguese glosses and sense items (P5137); the list included the Brazilian variants and alternatives (quatorze, dezesseis, dezessete, dezenove, cinza, cachorro, oi, tchau, obrigada) and nine phrases. irmã, filha and all phrases were not found as lemmas. The lexeme search (calls 2) found irmã and filha as forms of irmão (L447160) and filho (L677502), the greetings only as hyphenated nouns (bom-dia L1640417, boa-tarde L1646749, boa-noite L1626801), desculpar (L1665828) for desculpe, and nothing for até logo, até amanhã, com licença, por favor, de nada, obrigada, tchau or oi. The obrigado lexemes found have no interjection sense: the adjective L1608351 means obliged or grateful, and the noun L1664143 is a thank-you as a noun (\"agradecimento que se dá a alguém\"); the noun is cited as supporting evidence on the obrigado card. The full JSON of every lexeme found was fetched with API calls 3; for each card the lexeme of the right category was used (the numeral lexeme for 1–10, the noun lexeme for 0 and 11–100, the adjective lexeme for colours and adjectives, and so on)."@en ,
                 "Wikidata items. The items of the chosen senses, the number, day, month, colour and family items, the items found with the item search (calls 6: hello Q12068060, good afternoon Q19279334, good evening Q19279366, goodbye Q2506912, purple Q3257809), and thank you Q19279214, yes Q6452715 and no Q1814990, which the item search did not return among its first results and were taken from the records of the authored French deck (the number, day, month, colour and family items are also the ones that deck uses) were fetched with API calls 5 (labels and aliases in pt, pt-br, en, sv and mul). Where a sense's item is broader than the card, the narrower item was used for labels: irmão and irmã (sense item Q31184, sibling) use brother Q10861465 and sister Q595094; filho and filha (Q29514218, child) use son Q177232 and daughter Q308194; água (Q29053744, liquid water) uses water Q283."@en ,
                 "Phrases from the FSI course. For the phrases with no usable lexeme, the Internet Archive was searched for public-domain Foreign Service Institute Portuguese courses (call 8). The Portuguese Programmatic Course, Volume 1 (1974, in the ERIC archive) was the only FSI student text found; its OCR text was downloaded and saved as <scratch>/fsi-vol1.txt (call 9) and searched with grep (call 10), and each hit was read in context with the English column (lines and page markers are given in the evidence). bom dia, boa tarde and boa noite (Unit 15), obrigado (Unit 6), por favor (Unit 11), desculpe and de nada (Unit 16), muito prazer (Unit 9), sim (Unit 10) and não (Unit 20) were found. até logo, até amanhã and com licença were not found in the OCR text and are not cards. The course teaches Brazilian Portuguese. Each phrase used except muito prazer has an entry in Priberam's European-norm view (bom-dia, boa-tarde, boa-noite, obrigado, and por favor, de nada under favor and nada; desculpe under desculpar). muito prazer has no entry in Priberam or Portuguese Wiktionary; Priberam's page says only that its checker FLiP recognises it, and Portuguese Wiktionary gives prazer as an interjection said on being introduced, with a pronunciation for Portugal. The English backs follow the course's glosses where they match (good morning, good afternoon, good evening and good night, please, excuse me); \"thank you\" is the Wikidata label of Q19279214, and \"you’re welcome\", \"nice to meet you\" and \"sorry\" are the authors' usual renderings."@en ,
                 "Writing the cards. The front is the Portuguese word as in the lexeme's lemma (or the FSI phrase), lower case. Nouns are preceded by the definite article that shows their gender (o livro, a casa). Days and months are given without an article, with their gender in a note; colours and adjectives in the masculine singular with the feminine in a note; verbs in the infinitive. The back is the authors' own short English and Swedish rendering of the main sense: for nouns the bare noun, for verbs the infinitive (English \"to …\", Swedish \"att …\"), for numbers the digits (tagged zxx, as digits are in no language). English and Swedish follow the Wikidata labels where an item has them; otherwise they are the usual equivalents, chosen by the authors and checked in English Wiktionary and Folkets lexikon. Where a word has two everyday English or Swedish equivalents both are given (cidade: city, town; grande: big, large; falar: tala, prata; ir: gå, åka). English and Swedish equivalents and the facts in the notes not given by a content source are the authors' own knowledge of the languages, worded by the authors and only confirmed in the verification sources."@en ,
                 "Notes. A short note was added where it helps a beginner and a source confirms it: the gender of each day (with the short forms segunda, terça, quarta, quinta and sexta) and month, months in lower case since the 1990 Agreement, the feminine of each colour and adjective, um and dois with their feminines (and um as the article), the Brazilian spellings of 16, 17 and 19, quatorze, cem and cento, os pais and os irmãos, avô and avó, oi, tchau, obrigada, the times of day for the greetings, de nada as the reply to thanks, the literal meaning of muito prazer, não as \"not\", cão and cachorro, mulher as wife, a amiga, a mão and o dia, noite as the evening, novo as young, ser and estar, and the Swedish gå/åka and tack. The example phrases in the notes (um livro, não sei, sou sueco, estou em casa, estou cansado, ele é muito novo, en kaffe, tack) are the authors' own."@en ,
                 "Cross-checking. Every single-word headword was looked up in the Vocabulário Ortográfico Comum, Portuguese version (calls 13; spelling and word class or gender), in Priberam's dictionary (calls 14; European-norm reading, part of speech, gender and first definition), in Portuguese Wiktionary (calls 15; part of speech, gender template, first definition and inflection table) and in the Portuguese section of English Wiktionary (calls 16; English meaning). The phrases were looked up in Priberam, Portuguese Wiktionary and English Wiktionary (the VOC records single words, not phrases); muito prazer has an entry in none of the first two, so its parts were checked (prazer). The usage notes on cem and cento were checked in English Wiktionary's wikitext of both words (calls 17). Every Swedish back was checked in Folkets lexikon (calls 18). Python scripts built the evidence lines from the responses saved on 4 October 2026, so each quotation is what the source returned, with wiki markup and HTML removed; Priberam's page text was reduced to its European-norm reading (spans marked as Brazilian removed). Because Priberam's terms forbid reproducing any part of its website, its quotations were then cut (quality-control round 3) to the part of speech, gender, usage and norm labels and the first seven words or so of the definition, with \"…\" where cut and \"[…]\" for left-out parentheses, and its entries are named by headword rather than linked; other quotations are cut at about 160 characters and marked with \"…\". OCR quotations from the FSI course are given with the intended letters and the OCR reading in brackets."@en ,
                 "Machine checks. The dossier's Wikidata checks (the Portuguese, English and Swedish label or alias of each linked item that matches the card's text, and the mul digit label of each number) are re-run against live Wikidata by the builder on every build. Wikidata lexemes cannot be checked by the builder (it checks items only); the lexeme facts in the evidence were read from the API responses of 4 October 2026. The built deck was validated with the app's SHACL and DCAT-AP validators (scripts/validate_sources.ts)."@en ,
                 "Excluded: até logo, até amanhã and com licença (no lexeme and not in the public-domain FSI text), tchau and oi as cards of their own (informal alternatives, named in notes), words whose everyday form differs between Portugal and Brazil (o comboio/o trem, o autocarro/o ônibus, o pequeno-almoço/o café da manhã, a rapariga/a garota, castanho/marrom, cor-de-rosa/rosa, cor de laranja/laranja), the pronouns tu and você (whose use differs between the two varieties), and words with two equally common meanings that a single card cannot separate (o tempo: time or weather)."@en ,
                 "Selection: 120 cards: the cardinal numbers 0–20, the tens 30–90 and 100 (29); the seven days of the week; the twelve months; eight colours (vermelho, azul, verde, amarelo, preto, branco, cinzento, roxo); eight family members (mãe, pai, irmão, irmã, filho, filha, avô, avó); twelve greetings and polite phrases (olá, bom dia, boa tarde, boa noite, adeus, obrigado, por favor, de nada, desculpe, muito prazer, sim, não); 22 everyday nouns (house, water, bread, milk, apple, book, table, chair, door, window, dog, cat, city, street, school, man, woman, friend, hand, day, night, week); six common adjectives (grande, pequeno, bom, mau, novo, velho); and 16 common verbs in the infinitive (ser, estar, ter, ir, fazer, vir, falar, comer, beber, querer, poder, ver, ler, escrever, dormir, trabalhar). The list was chosen by the deck's authors for a first course, not copied from any frequency list or textbook. The deck follows European Portuguese, spelt by the 1990 Orthographic Agreement; words common to Portugal and Brazil were preferred, and where a card's word differs in Brazil the Brazilian form is in a note (dezesseis, dezessete, dezenove, o cachorro, oi). Nouns are given with the definite article (o, a); colours and adjectives in the masculine singular with the feminine in a note; days and months without an article and in lower case, with their gender in a note. Left out: até logo, até amanhã and com licença (no open content source gives them), tchau and oi as separate cards, words whose everyday form differs between Portugal and Brazil (train, bus, breakfast, girl, brown, pink, orange), the pronouns tu and você, and words with two equally common meanings (o tempo)."@en ,
                 "Licensing: The deck is CC0 1.0. Its content comes from two sources whose terms allow any reuse: Wikidata's lexemes and items (CC0 1.0: Portuguese lemmas, genders, forms, glosses and Portuguese, English and Swedish labels) and the Foreign Service Institute's Portuguese Programmatic Course, Volume 1 (1974), a work of the United States Government with no copyright protection under 17 U.S.C. § 105 (eight phrases with their meaning, and a check of sim, não and oi). The selection of words is the authors' own. The English and Swedish renderings not taken from Wikidata or the FSI course are the usual equivalents chosen by the authors; several are the same short stock glosses that English Wiktionary or Folkets lexikon give, as their source records say. Single words and fixed greetings such as \"nice to meet you\" or \"ingen orsak\" are facts of usage, not protectable expression, so this does not carry those sources' licences into the deck; no definition, example, comment or list was taken from them. The rule applied throughout: English and Swedish equivalents and the facts in the notes not given by a content source are the authors' own knowledge, worded by them and only confirmed in a verification source. Priberam's dictionary (all rights reserved; its terms forbid reproducing any part of the website and deep linking to it), the Vocabulário Ortográfico Comum (its publisher, the IILP, reserves all rights), Portuguese and English Wiktionary (CC BY-SA 4.0) and Folkets lexikon (CC BY-SA 2.5) were used for verification only. So no source requires attribution or share-alike for the deck, although all are credited in its provenance. The quotations in this provenance report are not covered by the deck's CC0 dedication or the repository's MIT licence: quotations from Priberam (© Priberam Informática, S.A., all rights reserved) are cut to grammatical labels and the first few words of a definition, the least that identifies the entry checked, and its entries are named by headword, not linked; quotations from the VOC (© IILP, all rights reserved) are its spelling and word-class labels; quotations from Portuguese and English Wiktionary are © Wiktionary contributors, CC BY-SA 4.0; quotations from Folkets lexikon are CC BY-SA 2.5; quotations from the FSI course are in the public domain; Wikidata data is CC0."@en .

<#review-0>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-04"^^xsd:date ;
    rdfs:label "Sources, licences and machine checks"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: All 120 cards: every Portuguese form, gender, English and Swedish meaning and note against the content sources and the verification sources; the builder's Wikidata checks."@en ,
                 "Outcome: Every card has evidence from a content source (Wikidata, or the FSI course for eight phrases) and at least two verification sources. The VOC (Portuguese version) and Priberam's European-norm entries agree with the spelling of every single-word headword; the gender shown on every noun, day and month card agrees with the Wikidata lexeme (its gender statement, or for irmão, irmã, filho, filha, gato and amigo the gender of its forms), the VOC and Priberam. English Wiktionary confirms every English back except two parts: \"sorry\" on desculpe (Wiktionary glosses desculpar as \"to forgive\" and \"to apologise\"; FSI gives \"excuse me\") and \"little\" on pequeno (Wiktionary: \"small\"; Wikidata's item small, Q16042592, has the alias \"little\"). Folkets lexikon or a Swedish Wikidata label confirms every Swedish back. The builder's Wikidata checks cover the Portuguese, English and Swedish labels or aliases of 99 linked items (256 checks), including the digit label of every number; they all pass. Discrepancies between sources and how they were resolved are listed below. No review round has been run yet."@en .

<#review-1>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-04"^^xsd:date ;
    rdfs:label "Factual accuracy"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: All 120 cards: Portuguese forms, genders, meanings, Brazilian variants and notes; the 256 Wikidata checks against the live API; the description's counts; the validator."@en ,
                 "Outcome: No factual errors. All 256 Wikidata checks match (33 through an alias); the description's counts are right; validate_sources.ts reports valid. One warning: muito prazer had no Portuguese-dictionary evidence although the method said every phrase was checked in Priberam and Portuguese Wiktionary. Three optional suggestions on the notes of cem, cinzento and estar. The warning and two suggestions were applied; one was declined."@en .

<#review-2>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-04"^^xsd:date ;
    rdfs:label "Language, translation and language tags"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: All 120 cards in Portuguese, English and Swedish; title, description, keywords and the language tags in the built TTL."@en ,
                 "Outcome: Language tags correct (120 @pt fronts, 29 @zxx number backs, 162 @en and 162 @sv texts, no untagged text); Portuguese spelling, articles and genders right; Swedish idiomatic; style consistent; description 103 words. Two optional polish points, both applied."@en .

<#review-3>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-04"^^xsd:date ;
    rdfs:label "Licensing, attribution and documentation"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: The licence of every source (terms pages fetched), the content/verification split, 17 cards' wording against the content sources, the FSI attribution, and the reproducibility of method and queries."@en ,
                 "Outcome: The CC0 licence is sound: the only content sources are Wikidata (CC0) and the 1974 FSI course (US government work). Two warnings: Priberam does have a terms page, which forbids reproducing any part of the website and deep linking, while the report quoted up to about 160 characters per entry and linked every entry; and the FSI usedFor overstated that the English backs are the course's glosses. Three suggestions: the VOC publisher's rights notice, the FSI title and editor, and unpublished helper scripts and a mixed query entry. All five were applied."@en .

<#review-4>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-04"^^xsd:date ;
    rdfs:label "Re-check of changed cards and a sample (facts, language, documentation)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: The five cards changed in rounds 1–3 (muito-prazer, cem, estar, desculpe, irmao) and every third card from zero to escrever, 42 cards in all, against sources fetched on 2026-10-04; the built TTL and report; the description, keywords, topics, method, queries and licensing; every fix logged in rounds 1–3."@en ,
                 "Outcome: No factual, language or language-tag error in the 42 cards; all Wikidata checks on them pass live; every fix claimed in rounds 1–3 is in the dossier and the outputs. One warning (the method cited Folkets lexikon as calls 17, which is query 18 since round 3 split the old query 14–16) and two suggestions (a duplicate English Wiktionary evidence entry on irmao, and about 140 Priberam deep links still recorded in query 14). All three were confirmed and fixed."@en .

<#review-5>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-04"^^xsd:date ;
    rdfs:label "Final full-deck review (facts and language)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: All 120 cards against the reviewer's own knowledge and the dossier's evidence; the description and its counts, keywords, study direction, language tags, and the three fixes logged in round 4."@en ,
                 "Outcome: No error found in the 120 cards; the description's counts, the keywords, the study direction and the language tags are right, and round 4's three fixes are in the dossier. One optional suggestion on the Swedish register of the back of por-favor (\"snälla\"), which was declined."@en .

<https://www.wikidata.org/>
    dcterms:title "Wikidata (Portuguese lexemes and items)" ;
    dcterms:creator "Wikidata contributors (Wikimedia Foundation)" ;
    dcterms:license <https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/> ;
    rdfs:comment "https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Wikidata:Licensing (read with action=raw) — \"All structured data (i.e. the main, Property, Lexeme, and EntitySchema namespaces) is released into the public domain under Creative Commons Zero.\""@en .

<https://archive.org/details/ERIC_ED096854>
    dcterms:title "Portuguese [Brazilian] Programmatic Course, Volume 1 (Foreign Service Institute, 1974), as scanned in the ERIC archive on the Internet Archive (ERIC ED096854)" ;
    dcterms:creator "Jack Lee Ulsh and associates; edited by Augustus A. Koski; Foreign Service Institute, U.S. Department of State" ;
    dcterms:license <https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/mark/1.0/> ;
    rdfs:comment "A work of the United States Government: the title page reads \"FOREIGN SERVICE INSTITUTE WASHINGTON, D.C. 1974\" and \"FOREIGN SERVICE INSTITUTE BASIC COURSE SERIES\", the front matter \"For sale by the Superintendent of Documents, U.S. Government Printing Office, Washington, D.C.\", and the preface is signed by the Dean of the FSI School of Language Studies (OCR: \"…R. Frith, Dean … chool of Language Studies Foreign Service Institute\"); the ERIC record gives \"Foreign Service (Dept. of State), Washington, D.C. Foreign Service Inst.\" as institution. 17 U.S.C. § 105 (https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/17/105): \"Copyright protection under this title is not available for any work of the United States Government\". The Internet Archive item's metadata (https://archive.org/metadata/ERIC_ED096854) states no licence. Recorded here with the Public Domain Mark: free of known copyright."@en .

<https://dicionario.priberam.org/>
    dcterms:title "Dicionário Priberam da Língua Portuguesa (online), European-norm view" ;
    dcterms:creator "Priberam Informática, S.A." ;
    rdfs:comment "Every page's footer: \"© 2026 Priberam Informática, S.A. Todos os direitos reservados\" (e.g. https://dicionario.priberam.org/). The company's Terms and Conditions (https://priberam.com/terms-conditions/, read on 2026-10-04 during quality-control round 3): \"Subject to clause 2.1, no part of the Website may be reproduced or stored in any other Website or included in any public or private electronic retrieval system or service without our prior written permission.\"; \"You may not permit, create unauthorized framing of, or deep linking to, the Website or the creation of derivative works thereof from any other website under your management or control.\"; \"All Intellectual Property Rights in the Website shall be owned by us absolutely.\" The dictionary's about page (https://dicionario.priberam.org/sobre.aspx) states no terms on reuse; robots.txt (https://dicionario.priberam.org/robots.txt) allows all user agents except four named crawlers (\"User-agent: * Allow: /\"). When the entries were looked up, only www.priberam.org/termos-e-condicoes/ was tried (it did not resolve) and the company's terms page above was not found; it was read after the look-ups. So Priberam is a verification source only: one page was fetched per headword, two seconds apart, nothing from it is in the cards, the quotations in this report are cut to the part of speech, gender, usage and norm labels and the first few words of a definition, and its entries are named by headword rather than linked."@en .

<https://voc.cplp.org/>
    dcterms:title "Vocabulário Ortográfico Comum da Língua Portuguesa (VOC), Portuguese national version (VOP: Vocabulário Ortográfico do Português, 2.ª edição)" ;
    dcterms:creator "Instituto Internacional da Língua Portuguesa (CPLP) and the national teams" ;
    rdfs:comment "No licence or terms of use are stated on the VOC site itself: the pages Apresentação (https://voc.cplp.org/index.php?action=apresent) and Ficha técnica (https://voc.cplp.org/index.php?action=ficha) were read and show only the choice of national version; there is no robots.txt (HTTP 404). Its publisher's site (https://iilp.cplp.org/, read on 2026-10-04 during quality-control round 3) has the footer \"Copyright © IILP 2024. Todos os direitos reservados.\"; its footer links \"Termos & Condições\" and \"Politica e Privacidade\" point to \"#\" (no page). Recorded as all rights reserved: verification only."@en .

